# Review process: how it fits together and one-time setup (GitLab)

This repository's review process follows how the Claude Code team does code review: most changes merge with at most one human in the loop, backed by risk-based reviewers, a merge queue and catching problems fast after the fact. The day-to-day rules are in the "Review process" section of `CLAUDE.md`; this page covers the layers, the one-time setup on GitLab ({{ gitlab.url }}, tier: {{ gitlab.tier }}) and what is and isn't defended.

## Layers

| Layer | What it is here | Files |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Before the MR | Run the checks locally and paste the output, `/code-review high`{{#if policy.securityPaths}}, `/security-review` for security review paths{{/if}} | `CLAUDE.md` |
| AI review on every push | The `ai-review` job: Claude Code reads the MR head read-only and answers with its findings; the process bot opens one thread per finding and posts a summary | `{{ instructionsPath }}`, `{{ securityPath }}`, `.gitlab/dev-process.gitlab-ci.yml` |
| Every AI comment answered | `{{ policy.names.gateContext }}` requires an answer to every thread {{ ai }} opened; "All threads must be resolved" blocks the merge until they are resolved | Same as above |
| Required checks | {{#each ci.requiredChecks}}`{{ this }}`{{#unless @last}}, {{/unless}}{{/each}}{{#if ci.releaseGate}}; release MRs also run `{{ ci.releaseGate.name }}`{{/if}} | `.gitlab-ci.yml` |
| People by risk | `{{ policy.names.gateContext }}`: path-based tier{{#if classifier}} + AI second opinion (can only raise it){{/if}} → how many people | `{{ configPath }}` |
| Merge queue | Add the `{{ policy.names.queueLabel }}` label: the bot re-runs CI on "latest `{{ defaultBranch }}` + this MR" (branch `{{ policy.names.queueContext }}/pr-N`) and merges when green and the gate still passes | `.gitlab/dev-process.gitlab-ci.yml` |
| Who can merge | `{{ defaultBranch }}` takes no pushes and only Maintainers merge; people are Developers, so only the bot merges, and only through the queue{{#if gitlab.premium}}; approval settings and Code Owner approval on top{{/if}} | `{{ engine }}/core/protection.mjs`, `.gitlab/CODEOWNERS` |
| Catching problems fast | Weekly on-call rotation, 48 hours of watching feedback after a release, incident reviews from a template | `docs/dev-process/on-call.md`, `docs/incident-review-template.md` |
{{#if loops.eval}}
| Incidents become the eval set | Weekly (and whenever the review instructions or cases change), Claude reviews each past incident's MR with the same instructions to see whether it catches the problem; clean MRs count false positives | `{{ policy.paths.evalCases }}`; schedule "dev-process: weekly review eval"; results in the issue labeled `{{ policy.names.evalLabel }}` |
{{/if}}
{{#if loops.metrics}}
| Measuring human time and noise | Weekly metrics: approvals and comments from others, waiting time, what happened to {{ ai }}'s comments, reverts and fixes | Schedule "dev-process: weekly metrics"; results in the issue labeled `{{ policy.names.metricsLabel }}` |
{{/if}}
{{#if loops.sweep}}
| Nightly sweep | Each night one security review path is read (read-only) for existing bugs; findings with a file and line become issues for a human to verify | Schedule "dev-process: nightly sweep"; issues labeled `{{ policy.names.sweepLabel }}` |
{{/if}}

GitLab runs no pipeline on a comment or an approval. The process bot therefore works in pipelines of `{{ defaultBranch }}`, started by a **webhook** (merge request and comment events → a pipeline trigger) and by a **schedule every 10 minutes** that catches up on anything the webhook missed. A passing gate on a queued MR wakes the queue at once; an MR pipeline finishing reaches the queue through the schedule. A pipeline of `{{ defaultBranch }}` uses `{{ defaultBranch }}`'s configuration and engine, so an MR cannot change how it is judged.

## Admin checklist (in this order)

1. **GitLab 17.11 or later.** The process uses the merge ref, MR diff versions and approval system notes as they work there.
1. **Runners** for the default branch's pipelines with a Docker executor (the bot's jobs use `node:{{ ci.node }}`){{#if ci.tagsYaml}}, and runners with the tags `tests` uses{{/if}}. Merge request pipelines run the MR's own code: don't give them runners that hold secrets.
1. **Run the setup** with your own token (Maintainer or Owner, `api` scope), first as a dry run:

   ```sh
   GITLAB_TOKEN=<your token> ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=<key> node {{ engine }}/cli/apply-protection.mjs --repo {{ repo }} --dry-run
   GITLAB_TOKEN=<your token> ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=<key> node {{ engine }}/cli/apply-protection.mjs --repo {{ repo }}
   ```

   It protects `{{ defaultBranch }}` (no pushes, merges by Maintainers, no force push), turns on "Pipelines must succeed" and "All threads must be resolved", lets only Maintainers start pipelines with variables{{#if gitlab.premium}}, sets the approval rules (reset on push, no author or committer approval) and Code Owner approval{{/if}}, creates the bot (a project access token, `api`, Maintainer, valid for a year) and stores it as `DEV_PROCESS_TOKEN` (protected, masked, environment `dev-process-bot`), stores `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` (protected, masked), and creates — as the bot — the pipeline trigger, the webhook and the schedules. Run it again any time: it only changes what differs.
1. **Self-managed: let the webhook reach the instance.** GitLab refuses webhooks to its own address by default. An admin allows it under Admin → Settings → Network → Outbound requests ("Allow requests to the local network from webhooks and integrations", or adds this host to the allowlist), then re-run the setup. Until then the 10-minute schedule alone moves things: after a sign-off or an approval the gate can take up to 10 minutes to update.
1. **Optional: the model.** A CI/CD variable `CLAUDE_REVIEW_MODEL` (e.g. a model id). Reviews are capped at {{ policy.aiReview.maxComments }} findings and {{ policy.aiReview.maxTurns }} turns (`aiReview` in `{{ configPath }}`).
1. **Every year: rotate the bot token** (Settings → Access tokens → Rotate) and update `DEV_PROCESS_TOKEN`. `doctor --remote` warns 30 days ahead; when it expires, the gate, the review and the queue all stop.
1. **A test MR**: open one, sign it off, add the `{{ policy.names.queueLabel }}` label and watch the `{{ defaultBranch }}` pipelines: the gate's note, the review threads and summary, the queue merging it.
1. **Then make people Developers.** On Free, anyone with Maintainer or Owner can merge past the gate. Once the test MR merged through the queue (the bot works), give the people who write code the Developer role and keep Owner to a few admins; the bot is then the only one merging. Do this last: if the bot does not work yet, nobody else could merge.
1. **Check**: `GITLAB_TOKEN=<your token> node {{ engine }}/cli/doctor.mjs --remote --repo {{ repo }}` — it names everyone above Developer.

## FAQ

- **The gate didn't update after my comment or approval.** If the webhook is blocked (see above), it updates within 10 minutes. Otherwise look at the latest `{{ defaultBranch }}` pipeline started by the trigger.
- **Claude didn't review.** A push starts the review within a minute; when a review is missing the gate's note quotes the reason (no key, the run failed, no answer). After fixing it, push again (an empty commit is fine); the 10-minute schedule also starts reviews for heads that have neither a review nor a notice after 25 minutes.
- **Claude is down or out of credit and it's urgent**: someone with write access comments `{{ policy.commands.waiver }} <head sha>`; the gate then needs {{ policy.aiReviewWaiverExtraApprovals }} more approval from someone else.
- **MRs from forks** are reviewed too (the review only reads their code), but the queue never takes them: their CI would run with this project's runners. Merge them through a branch of this project.

## Trust boundaries

- **An MR cannot change its own gate**: the bot's jobs run only in pipelines of `{{ defaultBranch }}`, with its configuration and engine; merge request pipelines never see `DEV_PROCESS_TOKEN` or `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` (protected variables). An MR that changes `.gitlab-ci.yml`, `.gitlab/**`, `CLAUDE.md` at any depth, `.claude/**`, `.mcp.json` or `.dev-process/**` is high tier.
- **Statuses are information**: any Developer can post a commit status, so nothing here trusts one that the bot did not write, and the queue reads CI results from the jobs themselves. What stops a merge is that only the bot can merge.
- **Claude's text cannot act**: the review job has no token, and before the bot posts anything it breaks mentions and references, removes quick actions (`/merge`, `/approve`, … at the start of a line) and secrets.
- **Not defended**: people with Maintainer or Owner on this project, and instance admins, can merge past the gate or change the protection; that is why people are Developers. A Maintainer who runs a pipeline with variables on `{{ defaultBranch }}` acts as the bot.
